## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) with tunable pore size and high surface area are one of promising electroactive materials for energy storage devices [1–4]. To solve the poor electrical conductivity and electroactivity of MOFs, a novel zeolitic imidazolate framework material 67 (ZIF67)-derived cobalt/nickel fluoride coupled with ammonia ions was firstly proposed as electroactive material of energy storage devices by our group [5]. The metal centers have large impacts on the energy storage ability. Roles of metal centers and its electrochemical behaviors have been studied in previous reports [6–10]. This ZIF67 derivative was synthesized using ammonia fluoride as structure-directing agent and fluorine source. The function of structure-directing agent can confine the growth of ZIF67 derivative to be small particle assembled sphere, and the function of fluorine source is to enhance the electrical conductivity. A specific capacitance $(\mathsf{C}_{\mathtt{F}})$ of $363.9\mathrm{F}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $20\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ was obtained for this ZIF67 derivative electrode, while the ZIF67 electrode only shows a $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ value of $3.5\mathsf{F}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}$ at the same condition [5]. Inspired by the large specific capacitance enhancement of this ZIF67 derivative prepared using ammonia fluoride, more modifications are worthy to applied for further improving its energy storage ability.
Two-dimensional material has attracted much attention on energy storage devices [11,12]. A novel group of two-dimensional transition metal carbides or carbonitrides, MXene, with high electrical conductivity, high specific capacitance and chemical stability, has been intensively applied on fabricating composites with pseudocapacitive materials to enhance electrical conductivity [13–16]. Chen et al. synthesized organ-like $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{CuCo}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ composite by dispersing $\mathsf{C u C o}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{4}$ nanoparticles on $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}$ nanosheets during hydrothermal process. The MXene $\boldsymbol{/\mathrm{CuCo}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}}$ electrode exhibited a higher specific capacity of $992.3\mathsf{C}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ than the $\mathsf{C u C o}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{4}.$ electrode $(675.2\mathrm{~C~g}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}$ MXene electrode $\begin{array}{r}{(92.3\mathsf{C\ }\mathsf{g}^{-1})}\end{array}$ [17]. He et al. synthesized $\mathsf{N i}_{1.5}\mathsf{C o}_{1.5}\mathsf{S}_{4}@\mathsf{T i}_{3}\mathsf{C}_{2}$ hybrid materials through in-situ growing $\mathsf{N i}_{1.5}\mathsf{C o}_{1.5}\mathsf{S}_{4}$ nanoparticles on $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ - nanoflakes during hydrothermal process. A specific capacity of 166.7 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ $600.1\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ with potential window of $1\ V$ ) at 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ was obtained for the $\mathsf{N i}_{1.5}\mathsf{C o}_{1.5}\mathsf{S}_{4}@\mathsf{T i}_{3}\mathsf{C}_{2}$ electrode [18]. Li et al. designed dodecaborate/MXene composites by modifying ammonium ion on MXene via ultrasonic treatment and inserting dodecaborate into inner surface of MXene via electrostatic adsorption. The dodecaborate/MXene electrode achieved a higher specific capacitance $({\mathsf{C}}_{\mathtt{F}})$ of $366\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ at $2{\mathrm{~mV~}}s^{-1}$ than that of MXene electrode $(43\mathrm{~F~g~}^{-1})$ ) [19]. Benefitted from the layered structure and high electrical conductivity of MXene, it is highly promising to incorporate MXene with the novel ZIF67 derivative proposed in our previous work to further enhance the energy storage ability. It is worthy to mention that heat or kinetic energy are generally required to promote uniform dispersion of pseudocapacitive materials into inner surface of MXene, as commonly applied in previous studies such as hydrothermal process [18] and ultrasonic treatment[19]. Herein, a simple room temperature solution process was firstly proposed to design self-assembled MXene/ZIF67 derivative composites without heat or kinetic energy. This simple, costeffective and energy-saving method highly promotes the development of this MXene/ZIF67 derivative composite as electroactive material of energy storage devices.
In this study, novel MXene/ZIF67 derivative composites with different MXene contents were synthesized using a simple one-step solution process at room temperature. Different amounts of cobalt and nickel ions were primarily dissolved in MXene solution as the self-assembly medium, and the other solution containing 2-methylimidazole (2-Melm) and ammonia fluoride was mixed with the metal ion-contained MXene solution under stirring for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . By using this very simple process, highly efficient MXene/ ZIF67 derivative composites can therefore be obtained. Higher specific surface area, pore volume and pore diameter were obviously obtained by incorporating higher amounts of MXene in composites. MXene substrate also constructs conductive networks to accelerate electron transportation and enhance electrical conductivity of MXene/ZIF67 derivative composites. The optimized MXene/ZIF67 derivative composite electrode exhibited a high $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ value of $1020.0\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ $170.0\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1};$ at $20\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ , while the ZIF67 derivative electrode only showed a $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ value of $574.2\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ $(95.7\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ at $20\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ . The symmetric energy storage device composed of optimized MXene/ZIF67 derivative composite electrodes achieved excellent Coulombic efficiency of $100\%$ during the whole process and a high $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ retention of $99\%$ after 6000 times repeated charge/discharge process.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Preparation of MXene
MXene was synthesized by exfoliating $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathsf{A l C}_{2}$ (MAX phase, Sigma-Aldrich) [20]. The solution for exfoliation was prepared by dissolving $_{2\mathrm{~g~}}$ of LiF $(\geq99.99\%$ trace metals basis, Sigma-Aldrich) in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of 9 M HCl $32\%$ , UNI-ONWARD). $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathsf{A l C}_{2}$ powder $(2{\textbf{g}})$ was slowly added into the solution to avoid violent exothermic reaction. The solution was then heated at $40~^{\circ}C$ under stirring for $30\mathrm{~h~}$ to etch Al layer in MAX phase and exfoliate $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathsf{A l C}_{2}$ to form MXene. To separate MXene dispersion from the acid, the solution was washed by deionized water (DIW) for several times until pH value reaching 7. Finally, the MXene solution was sonicated for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ to further exfoliate and disperse MXene in the solution.
## 2.2. Preparation of ZIF67-derived nickel cobalt compounds by $N H_{4}F$ (NCNF)
The NCNF was synthesized using a simple solution process according to our previous work [5]. In brief, two solutions were mixed at room temperature under stirring at $200~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ One solution was prepared via dissolving $6.6\ \mathrm{\mmol}$ of cobalt nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , $99.0\%$ , Showa) and $3.3\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of nickel nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , $99.0\%$ Acros) in $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of methanol ( $95\%$ , Aecore). The other solution was prepared via dissolving $120~\mathrm{\mmol}$ of 2-Melm $99\%$ Acros) and $135\mathrm{\mmol}$ of ammonium fluoride $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{4}F}$ , $98.0\%$ , Sigma Aldrich) in $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of methanol. After reaction, precipitates were collected by filtration. Finally, the products were washed using methanol and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ overnight.
## 2.3. Preparation of MXene and NCNF composites
The MXene and NCNF composites (MXene/NCNF) were also synthesized by using the simple solution process, as shown in Scheme 1. Two solutions were mixed at room temperature under stirring at 200 rpm for $24\ \mathrm{h}.$ . One solution was prepared by dissolving MXene solution, 6.6 mmol of $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $3.3\mathrm{\mmol}$ of ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ in $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of methanol. The other solution was prepared via dissolving $120~\mathrm{\mmol}$ of 2-Melm and $135\mathrm{\mmol}$ of ammonium fluoride in $100\ \mathrm{mL}$ of methanol. The 1.0, 2.5, 5.0, 10, 20, 30 and $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of MXene solution were added in the first solution for synthesizing composites, which were nominated as MN1.0, MN2.5, MN5.0, MN10, MN20, MN30 and MN40, respectively. After reaction, precipitates were collected by filtration. Finally, the products were washed using methanol and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ overnight.
## 2.4. Fabrication of energy storage electrodes
The Ni foam was used as the flexible substrate for fabricating electrodes. The Ni foam was cleaned according to our previous work [21]. The electrode was fabricated by coating the paste on Ni foam and drying the electrode at $60~^{\circ}C$ overnight. The paste was prepared by mixing $70~\mathrm{mg}$ of active material, carbon black and polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE, Polysciences, Inc.) with the ratio of 7: 2: 1 in $1.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of mixed solvent composed of DIW and ethanol (volume ratio of 1 to 1) at room temperature under stirring for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The symmetric energy storage device was assembled using the optimized MXene/NCNF electrodes and electrolyte of $3~\mathrm{M}$ KOH. This two-electrode system was developed by inserting the optimized MXene/NCNF electrodes in a beaker containing $3\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH. The distance of two electrodes is set as close as possible. Electrolyte was resupplied after every 500 times of charge/discharge process in measuring cyclic performance.
## 2.5. Measurements and characterizations
The morphology of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathsf{A l C}_{2}$ , MXene and MXene/NCNF was observed using field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM, Nova NanoSEM 230, FEI, Oregon, USA) and field emission gun transmission microscope (FEG-TEM, Philips Tecnai F30). Compositions of NCNF and MXene/NCNF were examined via X-ray diffraction patterns (XRD, X’Pert3 Powder, PANalytical) and X-ray and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, VG Scientific ESCALAB 250) with Al Ka radiation. The surface area and pore properties were determined by the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) method using nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms. Electrochemical performances of NCNF and MXene/NCNF electrodes were measured in a three-electrode system using Pt as counter electrode and $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ as reference electrode. The cyclic voltammetry (CV), Galvanostatic charge/discharge (GC/D) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) were carried out using the potentiostat/- galvanostat instrument equipped with an FRA2 module (PGSTAT 204, Autolab, Eco–Chemie, the Netherlands). The EIS measurement was conducted at open-circuit potential with frequency ranged from $0.01~\mathrm{Hz}$ to $100~\mathrm{kHz}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Physical analysis of NCNF, MXene and their composites
The morphology of MXene, NCNF and MXene/NCNF was examined using SEM images, as shown in Fig. 1. The formation of MXene from the exfoliation of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathsf{A l C}_{2}$ was firstly investigated. Fig. 1(a) and (b) respectively shows the SEM images of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathsf{A l C}_{2}$ and MXene. The $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathsf{A l C}_{2}$ presents huge bulk structures with size of 1 to $2~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ After etching by acid, MXene shows clearly separated layered structures. The TEM image of MXene in the insert of Fig. 1(b) also indicates the success of exfoliation. The SEM image of NCNF was shown in Fig. 1(c), which presents sheet-assembled sphere structure with diameter of around $5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . The TEM image of NCNF was shown in the insert of Fig. 1(c). The folded layers suggest the assembly of sphere by sheets. On the other hand, the morphology of MXene/NCNF was examined. Fig. 1(d)-(j) respectively show the SEM images of MN1.0, MN2.5, MN5.0, MN10, MN20, MN30 and MN40. All the MXene and NCNF composites show folded sheets structures. The formation of folded sheets was explained by illustrating the self-assembly process. Fig. 1(k) shows the schematic illustration of the MXene/NCNF self-assembly process. At the first stage, cobalt and nickel ions adsorbed on surface of MXene in solution. After mixing with 2-Melm and ammonia fluoride, metal fluorides and ZIF67 were formed on the surface of MXene. At the same time, several ammonia ions distributed around the composites. At this stage, ZIF67 is not completely formed, since 2-Melm is not fully attached by metal ions. The unsaturated 2-Melm can combine with another metal ions adsorbed on surface of another MXene. As a result, two MXene-based composites are connected using 2- Melm as bridge. By continuing this process, more and more MXene/NCNF is combined to form larger composites. On the other hand, with higher amounts of MXene in the composites, more sheet-assembled spheres appeared. The MN1.0 and MN2.5 only present folded layers without assembly of spheres. The MN5.0, MN10, MN20, MN30 and MN40 show obvious sheet-assembled spheres. Thicker sheets and loosely packed assembly were found for the composites with more MXene. Fig. 1(l) shows the schematic illustration for the morphology differences of MXene/NCNF with different ratios of MXene. The composites with less MXene have more cobalt and nickel ions adsorbed on single MXene sheet, leading to more 2-Melm acting as bridge located between single MXene/NCNF. The MXene sheet is more likely to arrange in parallel directions due to large amounts of 2-Melm bridges connected inbetween. As a result, MN1.0 and MN2.5 with smaller MXene ratios and hence more 2-Melm bridges connected in-between form folded sheets instead of sheet-assembled spheres. The MN5.0, MN10, MN20, MN30 and MN40 with larger MXene ratios and hence less 2-Melm bridges connected in-between form sheetassembled sphere structure, since the growing direction of sheet is controlled by single or few 2-Melm with constant bonding angles.
The composition of MXene, NCNF and MXene/NCNF was examined by XRD patterns. The compositions of MAX and MXene were firstly compared to examine the exfoliation extent. Fig. 2(a) shows XRD patterns of MAX and MXene. Three obvious peaks between $35^{\circ}$ to $45^{\circ}$ were found in patterns of MAX and MXene, but the peak intensities of MXene are much smaller. Also, the main peak at $9.5^{\circ}$ corresponding to (002) facet in the MAX pattern cannot be observed in the MXene pattern. These phenomena indicate the successful exfoliation of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathsf{A l C}_{2}$ to form MXene. Similar results were reported in previous works [22,23]. On the other hand, Fig. 2(b) shows XRD patterns of MXene, NCNF, MN1.0, MN2.5, MN5.0, MN10, MN20, MN30 and MN40. The NCNF pattern shows the main peak at $11.3^{\circ}$ and smaller peaks at $23.0^{\circ}$ , $33.8^{\circ}$ , $38.2^{\circ}$ , $45.8^{\circ}$ and $58.6^{\circ}$ . The XRD patterns of MXene/NCNF prepared using higher MXene amounts in solution show smaller peaks intensities corresponding to NCNF, indicating that tuning MXene amounts in solution can efficiently control the MXene ratios in composites. Also, the main peak of NCNF at $11.3^{\circ}$ shifted toward smaller degrees for the composites with higher MXene amounts, indicating that more MXene in composites can increase the d-spacing of materials. The MN1.0 and MN2.5 show similar intensities of the main peak at $11.3^{\circ}$ . However, the main peak shows reduced intensity and shifted toward smaller degrees for the composites with higher MXene amounts. The result suggests that the composition of MXene/NCNF is dominated by MXene when more than $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of MXene was used for synthesis. This phenomenon can also be verified by observing the morphology of composites. The MN1.0 and MN2.5 show folded sheets without sphere assembly, whereas the sheet-assembled sphere was clearly observed for MN5.0, MN10, MN20, MN30 and MN40. The MXene/NCNF composites synthesized using more than $5\mathrm{mL}$ of MXene presented different growing control parameters from those prepared using less than $5\mathrm{mL}$ of MXene. In addition, the Raman spectra of MXene, NCNF, MN1.0, MN2.5, MN5.0, MN10, MN20, MN30 and MN40 were shown in Figure S1 in the supporting information (SI) for further examining the composition. The Raman spectrum of MXene is divided into two regions. The region from 200 to $500~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ shows in-plane $\left(\operatorname{E}_{\mathtt{g}}\right)$ vibrations of surface groups attached to Ti atoms. This region is merely affected by surface atoms. It could be used to investigate surface chemistry of MXene and changes by chemical or electrochemical reactions. The region from 580 to $760~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is assigned to carbon vibrations (both $\mathtt{E_{g}}$ and $\mathsf{A}_{1\mathrm{g}}\overline{{\mathbf{\Omega}}}$ . This region is commonly used to examine surface groups in in-situ electrochemical studies [24]. The Raman spectrum of NCNF merely presents one characteristic peak at $687~{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ . The characteristic peaks of MXene are invisible in the Raman spectra of composites, suggesting that the surface groups and atom vibrations in MXene are not obvious after combining with NCNF. Extra peak located at 700 to $750~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ appeared in the Raman spectra of composites, but the characteristic peak of NCNF at $687~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ cannot be observed in these spectra. Therefore, it is inferred that these extra peaks are originated from the peak at $687~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ shifted toward higher wavenumber. Shifting of peaks towards higher wavenumber indicates the shortening of chemical bond length of molecules [25]. These phenomena are more obvious for MN2.5, MN20 and MN30. According to the different sorts of morphology and XRD peak shifts, the composites can be separated into two groups. One group contains MN1.0 and MN2.5. The other group includes MN5.0 and those composed of higher MXene ratios. Based on the same sort, the composites with higher MXene ratios presents shorter chemical bond length of molecules. This phenomenon is caused by larger attractions between atoms in NCNF surrounded by more MXene in environments.
Moreover, the XPS spectra of MXene, NCNF and MN2.5 were measured to probe the chemical functionalities. Fig. 3(a) shows the survey spectra of MXene, NCNF and MN2.5. The Ti and F peaks are observed in MXene spectrum, suggesting the composition of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ . In the NCNF spectrum, clear Co, Ni, O, N and F peaks are presented, proving the successful synthesis of bimetallic compound and incorporation of nitrogen-contained 2-Melm and $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}.$ . The MN2.5 spectrum shows similar peaks as NCNF, due to the very small ratio of MXene. This phenomenon implies the dominated role of NCNF in MN2.5. Fig. 3(b)-(d) shows the high-resolution XPS spectra. Fig. 3(b) shows the F 1 s spectra of MXene, NCNF and MN2.5. Three characteristic peaks are fitted in F 1 s spectrum of MXene, including limited $\mathsf{A l F_{x}}$ peak at $688.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ , main peak of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2\mathrm{-}\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{x}}$ at $688.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ and ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-Ti-F_{x}}}$ at $686.7\ \mathrm{eV}.$ . The single fluorine peak dominated as $\mathsf{F}^{-}$ at $690.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ was clearly found in the F 1 s spectra of both NCNF and MN2.5. This result indicates that fluorine in MN2.5 is mainly contributed from NCNF, and the environments and fluorine bonding are almost unchanged after incorporating with MXene. Fig. 3(c) and (d) respectively shows the Co 2p and $\mathsf{N i2p}$ spectra of NCNF and MN2.5. The $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ and $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ peaks followed by satellite peaks are characterized in both spectra. In Co $\mathsf{2p}$ spectra, more ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ ions occupied in $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ and $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ peaks, whereas the precursor of $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ was used for synthesis. The oxidation of $C0^{2+}$ to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ implies the electron transfer between metal ions and MXene. Similar configurations of $\mathtt{C o2p}$ and Ni 2p spectra were observed for NCNF and MN2.5, again implying the dominated role of NCNF in MN2.5. The O 1 s spectra of NCNF and MN2.5 in
Fig. 3(e) show single metal and oxygen bonding. This result suggests that MXene nearly shows no bonding with oxygen in NCNF. From the detailed composition analysis of XPS spectra, it is inferred that MN2.5 is mainly composed of NCNF, and the formation of NCNF in MN2.5 is almost unchanged by the connected MXene even if cobalt and nickel ions are adsorbed on MXene at the first stage of synthesis. MXene plays as the conductive medium but has limited influences on chemical environments of NCNF.
Surface area and pore structure of electroactive materials have large impacts on the electrochemical performance, since reactions and ion adsorption mainly occur at interface between electroactive material and electrolyte. It is significant to investigate the surface properties of NCNF and MXene/NCNF. The nitrogen adsorption/ desorption spectra and pore distribution spectra were respectively shown in Figure S2 and Figure S3 in SI. The BET surface area $(S_{\mathrm{BET}})$ , pore volume $(\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{pore}})$ and diameter $(\mathrm{{D_{pore}})}$ of NCNF, MN1.0, MN2.5, MN5.0, MN10, MN20, MN30 and MN40 were listed in Table 1 for clearer comparison. It is observed that all MXene/NCNF show higher $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ and $\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{pore}}$ values than those of NCNF, and these values increase significantly for the MXene/NCNF with more MXene. These phenomena indicate that MXene plays very important roles on the surface properties of MXene/NCNF. The incorporation of MXene can largely enhance the specific surface area and pore volume, which favor for the contacts between electroactive material and electrolyte. On the other hand, MN1.0 and MN2.5 show smaller $\scriptstyle\mathbf{D_{pore}}$ than that of NCNF. However, MN5.0, MN10, MN20, MN30 and MN40 show larger $\scriptstyle\mathbf{D_{pore}}$ than NCNF, and the $\scriptstyle\mathbf{D_{pore}}$ increases for the MXene/NCNF composed of more MXene. The MN1.0 and MN2.5 show folded sheets morphology. As the MXene amount increases, the higher compression due to more tightly folded sheets leads to smaller pore diameter. However, the MN5.0, MN10, MN20, MN30 and MN40 presents sheet-assembled sphere morphology. Enhancing the MXene amounts in composites induces thicker sheets and looser package, leading to larger $\mathsf{D}_{\mathsf{p o r e}}$ for the composites with higher MXene ratios.
## 3.2. Electrochemical analysis of NCNF and MXene/NCNF
The electrochemical performance of NCNF and MXene/NCNF was further examined using CV and ${\sf G C}/{\sf D}$ curves. Fig. 4(a) shows the CV curves of NCNF and MXene/NCNF measured at $20\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ . The CV curve of the NCNF electrode shows one couple of obvious redox peaks with the oxidation and reduction potentials at 0.32 and $0.16\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}}$ respectively. These redox peaks were considered as overlapped peaks contributed from the redox reactions of cobalt fluoride and nickel fluoride. The CV curves of MXene/NCNF electrodes present similar rectangular shapes with one oxidation peak at $0.18\mathrm{~V}_{\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}}$ and two reduction peaks at 0.05 and $0.35\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}}$ , which is very different from that of NCNF electrode. The shape of CV curves for MXene/NCNF suggests the pseudocapacitive behavior [26,27]. This phenomenon suggests the high impact of MXene on redox behavior, because MN1.0 electrode presents similar redox behavior as MN40 electrode with 40-fold of MXene. In addition, MXene/NCNF electrodes with different MXene ratios show distinct variations on oxidation and reduction peaks of CV curves. At the potential from 0.27 to $0.43\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}}$ , apparent differences on oxidation peaks were observed, as enlarged at the right upper portion of this figure. The MN1.0, MN2.5 and MN5.0 electrodes present limited oxidation peak. The MN10 and MN20 electrodes show two small separated peaks, and MN20 electrode shows smaller potential difference of these peaks. The MN30 and MN40 electrodes present only one distinct oxidation peak, which is regarded as the overlapped peak. It is inferred that MXene possesses two obvious oxidation peaks at this region. The invisible oxidation peaks in the CV curves of MN1.0, MN2.5 and MN5.0 electrodes are due to the small amounts of MXene. The obvious oxidation peaks in the CV curves of MN10 and MN20 electrodes suggests the suitable amounts of MXene in NCNF, which can possibly display the features of both MXene and NCNF. The one overlapped peak in the CV curves of MN30 and MN40 electrodes is probably due to the enlarged contribution of MXene by incorporating in NCNF. On the other hand, in the right lower figure, the reduction peaks located at similar potentials for MN1.0, MN2.5, MN5.0 and MN10 and MN20 electrodes. The MN30 and MN40 electrodes shows higher reduction potentials, leading to smaller peak separation of reduction peaks. This phenomenon is like their oxidation peaks located at closer potentials. On the other hand, the $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ value was calculated using CV curves according to Equation (1) as follows [28].
$$
\mathsf{C F}=\frac{\int\mathrm{IdV}}{\Delta\mathsf{V}\cdot\mathsf{v}}
$$
where $\textstyle\int\operatorname{IdV}$ is integrated area of CV curve, $\Delta\mathsf{V}$ is the potential window (V), and $\upnu$ is the scan rate $(\mathsf{V}\mathsf{s}^{-1})$ . The $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ values of NCNF and MXene/NCNF electrodes were listed in Table 2. All the MXene/NCNF electrodes show higher $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ values than that of NCNF electrode except for MN40 electrode, owing to the enhanced electrical conductivity, surface area and pore volume by incorporating sheet-like MXene in composites. The MN40 electrode with the highest MXene amount shows a similar $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ value as that of NCNF electrode. Although the electrical conductivity and surface properties are improved by adding MXene, the capacitive metal fluorides in NCNF could be insufficient in MN40 due to the overloaded MXene. Among MXene/NCNF electrodes, the MN2.5 electrode shows the highest $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ value of $1020.0\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ $170\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1})}$ at $20\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ , which is almost two folds of that for the NCNF electrode. The function of MXene is to enhance the surface area and pore volume, which was verified using nitrogen adsorption/desorption spectra. The 2-dimensional plane of MXene can provide growing supports for NCNF, which can expose more surface area and develop more well-defined pore structure. The composite with more MXene has higher surface area and pore volume, which is beneficial for electrolyte contacts. However, the morphology changed from folded layers (MN1.0 and MN2.5) to sheet-assembled sphere (MN5.0 to MN40) when higher MXene amounts were used for synthesis. The folded layers are expected to expose more surface to electrolyte than sheet-assembled spheres, since the inner surface of sheet-assembled sphere may not be totally accessible by electrolyte. On the other hand, the $\mathsf{G C/D}$ curves at $2\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ for NCNF and MXene/NCNF electrodes were shown in Fig. 4(b). All electrodes show high symmetry of its charge and discharge curves, suggesting the high charge/discharge reversibility. The GC/D curves show several small plateaus, which are attributed from redox reactions. This phenomenon is consistent with the several peaks in CV curves. The IR drop of NCNF and MXene/NCNF electrodes is smaller than $0.02{\mathrm{~V}}_{;}$ , due to the small electrolyte resistance by the sheet-like structure. The $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ value of NCNF and MXene/NCNF electrodes was calculated using GC/D curves according to Equation (2) as follows [29].
$$
\mathsf{C F}=\frac{I\cdot t}{\Delta V}
$$
where I is current density $(\mathsf{A}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1})$ for measuring $\mathsf{G C/D}$ curves and t is the discharge time (s). The $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ values of NCNF and MXene/NCNF electrodes were listed in Table 2 for clearer comparison. The MXene/NCNF electrodes show higher $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ values than that of NCNF electrode except for MN40 electrode. The MN2.5 electrode shows the highest $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ value of $503.2\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ $83.9\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}.$ , which is more than 1.5 folds of the $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ value for NCNF electrode $(310.4\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $51.7\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ ), again indicating the best electrochemical performance of MN2.5 electrode due to the folded sheets structure, high electrical conductivity, and excellent surface properties.
Moreover, the Nyquist plot of MXene/NCNF electrodes was measured to examine resistances in the system, as shown in Fig. 4(c). The enlarged plot at high frequency region and equivalent circuit were inserted in this figure for closer observation. The Nyquist plot of NCNF electrode was shown in Figure S4 in SI for comparison. The series resistance $(\mathsf{R}_{\mathsf{S}})$ is the total resistance from electrode, electrolyte and interface. The $\mathtt{R}_{\mathtt{S}}$ value was estimated from the $\mathbf{x}$ -intercept. The charge-transfer resistance $(\mathsf{R}_{\mathsf{C T}})$ is the main resistance at the electrode/electrolyte interface, which can be evaluated by the diameter of semicircle at high frequency region. The CPE is the constant phase element, which is a nonintuitive circuit element that was discovered while looking at the response of real-world systems. The $z_{\mathrm{w}}$ is the Warburg diffusion element, which is related to the resistance of electrolyte. [30–32] The $\mathtt{R}_{\mathtt{S}}$ and $\mathsf{R}_{\mathsf{C T}}$ values of NCNF and MXene/NCNF electrodes were listed in Table 2. The largest $\mathtt{R}_{\mathtt{S}}$ value of $1.70\ \Omega$ and $\mathsf{R}_{\mathsf{C T}}$ value of $\mathbf{0.75}\ \Omega$ were found for NCNF electrodes, while MXene/NCNF electrodes show $\mathtt{R}_{\mathtt{S}}$ value smaller than $0.5\Omega$ and $\mathsf{R}_{\mathsf{C T}}$ value less than $0.12\ \Omega$ . The greatly reduced resistances of the MXene/NCNF electrodes comparing to those of NCNF electrode are resulted from the incorporation of highly conductive MXene. The enhancements on surface area and pore volume for the composites with MXene incorporation also favor for the decreased resistances.
Last, the high-rate charge/discharge performance of NCNF and MXene/NCNF electrodes was investigated by measuring CV curves and GC/D curves at different conditions. The CV curves at different scan rates and GC/D plot at various current densities of NCNF and MXene/NCNF electrodes were respectively shown in Figure S5 and S6 in SI. The relations between scan rates and current densities to $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ values were plotted in Fig. 4(d) and (e), respectively. The $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ retention was also calculated and listed in Table 2 for clearer comparison. All the electrodes show very high $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ retentions calculated from CV and GC/D curves, implying the excellent high-rate charge/ discharge performance of NCNF and MXene/NCNF composites. The MN2.5 electrode shows very high $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ retentions of $85.8\%$ and $99.0\%$ when scan rate increased from 10 to $50\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ and current density increased from 2 to 4 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . The excellent high-rate charge/discharge performance along with the high $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ value and small resistances of MN2.5 electrode promote its efficient electroactive material behavior.
## 3.3. Electrochemical analysis of symmetric energy storage device composed of MN2.5 electrodes
To have more practical application, symmetric energy storage device (SESD) was assembled using MN2.5 electrodes. Fig. 5(a) shows CV curves measured using different potential windows. All CV curves are highly overlapped at oxidation region, but reduction peak current density increases in CV curves in larger potential windows. This phenomenon is caused by activation of material especially in high potential regions. No obvious water redox reaction occurred at high potential region, but the integrated area of CV curves at potential higher than $0.5\mathrm{V}$ is limited. Therefore, the optimized potential window of $1.0\mathrm{~V~}$ was determined. Based on this potential window, CV curves were measured using different scan rates, as shown in Fig. 5(b). The CV curves remained highly similar shapes at all scan rates. The oxidation and reduction peaks of all CV curves are almost located at similar potential of $0.0\mathrm{V}$ and $-0.05\mathrm{V}$ , respectively. Also, the peak separation of CV curves is less than $0.05{\mathrm{~V}}_{\cdot}$ , which favors for an efficient energy storage device with high charge/discharge reversibility. Similarly, GC/D curves were measured using different potential windows, as shown in Fig. 5 (c). All curves are highly symmetric, suggesting high reversibility in all potential windows. At the potential higher than $0.5\mathrm{V}$ , charge and discharge times are limited. Therefore, the optimized potential window of $1.0\mathrm{V}$ was again confirmed. Fig. 5(d) shows GC/D curves measured using different current densities. The high symmetry was again observed for all curves. The Ragone plot was evaluated based on Fig. 5(d) to investigate the relation between energy and power densities, as shown in Fig. 5(e). Energy density (E) and power density (P) were calculated with Equation (3) and (4) as follows[33].
$$
\mathsf{E}=\frac{1}{2}\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{F}}\Delta\mathsf{V}^{2}
$$
$$
\mathbf{P}={\frac{\mathrm{E}}{\mathrm{t}}}
$$
where DV (V) is potential window and t (s) is discharge time. The highest energy density of 2.40 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ was obtained at power density of $50\ \mathrm{~W~}\ \mathrm{kg}^{-1}.$ and at the highest power density of $250\mathsf{W}\mathsf{k g}^{-1}$ the energy density still maintained $1.43\mathrm{Wh}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ . Last but not the least, cycling stability of the SESD was examined. Fig. 5 (f) shows relations between $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ retention and Coulombic efficiency to the cycle number. The GC/D curves at the first and last five cycles were also presented in the insert of this figure. The cycling stability was measured in 6000 times repeated charge/discharge process at $0.3\mathrm{~A~g}^{-1}$ . The activation is very serious for this device, due to the folded layer structure which possesses numerous tiny and folded pores. By repeatedly diffusing and migrating of electrolyte ions, the pores in the folded layer could be widened and more surface area could be exposed to electrolyte. Activation is serious between the 2600th and the 4600th cycle. A very high $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ retention of $99\%$ was obtained after 6000 cycles. The Coulombic efficiency of $100\%$ was also achieved during the whole charge/discharge process. The high cycling stability supports the excellent energy storage ability of this SESD composed of optimized MXene/NCNF electrodes newly proposed in this work. The activation process is found to be significant for this system. Figure S7 in SI shows the CV curves and GC/D curves of this device measured without activation. The much poorer electrochemical performance indicates the necessity of activation of the electroactive material with folded sheets structure.
The excellent energy storage ability of MXene/NCNF has been carefully discussed. To have fair evaluation of its electrochemical performance, the energy storage ability along with the synthesis method of MXene-based composites reported in previous studies were compared, as shown in Table 3. The gold nanoparticle, carbon materials and metal compounds combined with MXene were applied as electroactive material of energy storage devices. Several methods were used for fabricating MXene composites, such as selfreduction, vacuum filtration, hydrothermal process and electrodeposition. Comparing to these methods, the one-step solution process proposed in the present work is the most cost-effective and time-saving method. Only the precursor salts and stirrer were required to carry out the synthesis in $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Regarding to the electrochemical performance, the $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ value of $1020.0\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $20\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ of the optimized MXene/NCNF electrode synthesized in the present work is much larger than most of the $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ values reported in previous literatures. The cycling stability with $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ retention of $99\%$ after 6000 cycles achieved in this work is also much better than most of the reported values in previous literatures. In all, the novel MXene/
NCNF proposed in the present work is easy to synthesize and performs very well. It is very promising to study this system in wider viewpoints to further enhance its energy storage ability.
## 4. Conclusions
The MXene and NCNF composites were firstly synthesized using a simple one-step solution process at room temperature and applied as electroactive materials of energy storage device. Folded layers were converted to sheet-assemble spheres when more MXene were incorporated in composites. The larger specific surface area, pore volume and pore diameter were obtained with higher amounts of MXene in composites. The MN2.5 electrode presents a $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ value of $1020.0\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ $170.0\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1};$ at $20\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ while the NCNF electrode only shows a $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ value of $574.2\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ $(95.7\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}.$ ) at $20\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ . The previous reports studying composites of MXene and metal compounds commonly presented $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ values less than $1000\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Symmetric energy storage device with MN2.5 electrodes shows the highest energy density of 2.40 Wh $\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at the power density of $50\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ , and excellent cyclic ability with Coulombic efficiency of $100\%$ and $C_{\mathrm{F}}$ retention of $99\%$ after 6000 times repeated charge/discharge process. Other than simply controlling the ratio of MXene and NCNF, the reaction temperature and duration are also important factors to determine the energy storage ability of electroactive materials. It is expected to further enhance the electrochemical performance of MXene/NCNF composites via refining the experimental process in the future.
